  "id": "DrugBank_DB06724 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Carbonate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis through a series of interconnected pathways involving hypercalcemia and vitamin D metabolites. The evidence suggests that Calcium Carbonate is associated with Hypercalcemia Disease, which in turn is linked to Calcifediol and Calcitriol, both of which are involved in the regulation of calcium homeostasis and mineral deposition. These mechanisms are consistent with the known role of hypercalcemia in promoting calcium deposition in the kidneys, a hallmark of Nephrocalcinosis. The presence of these intermediates in the pathway supports the hypothesis that Calcium Carbonate contributes to the development of Nephrocalcinosis by influencing calcium metabolism and renal calcium deposition. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of hypercalcemia and vitamin D signaling in the pathophysiology of Nephrocalcinosis, with Calcium Carbonate acting as a mediator in this process.",
